// Low End - guitar, bass and a kit on one riff, with three synths layered on as it goes.
//
// Written in bass notation: e2 is the open low E, sounding an octave below
// the page, which is also how Standard Bass names its samples. The library
// is a five-string, so b1 is there, and the riff uses the notes below E for
// weight.
//
// How it is built, because a riff that flows is not an accident:
//
// Harmony first. Eight bars, two four-bar phrases, one chord a bar:
//
//   Em  C   D   B7   |   Em  C   Am  B7  -> Em
//   i   VI  VII V    |   i   VI  iv  V
//
// B is the major V from E harmonic minor. Its D# is the leading tone, a half
// step under E, and that pull home is most of what makes this sound like
// Megami Tensei and not generic rock. The first phrase ends on V, a question;
// the second ends on V too, but the turnaround climbs chromatically into the
// downbeat of E, so the loop answers itself every time it comes round.
//
// One motif. Every bar opens with the same two-beat cell on its chord root:
//
//   ROOT (dotted eighth, accented)  ghost (muted sixteenth)  OCTAVE (short pop)  root
//
// Repetition is what lets a listener lock on. Beats 3 and 4 then change every
// bar, and they are never decoration: they walk to the next chord's root, by
// step or by half step. Bar 1 ends on D going to C, bar 2 on C# going to D,
// bar 3 on C going down to B. That walk is the flow - every bar leans into the
// next one instead of stopping at the barline.
//
// Slides. A slide is how a bass player gets between two frets without
// picking again, so it belongs where the line already moves by step into a
// new chord - it joins the two bars into one gesture. It goes on four of the
// eight changes, not all of them: D to C, C to B, F# to E and Bb to A, all
// sliding down into the new root. The chromatic lead-ins that climb (C# to D,
// the final C C# D D# to E) stay picked, because those are meant to push, and
// a slide would smooth them away. One scoop lifts into the high e3, and the
// ending falls off the neck.
//
// Contour. Phrase one stays low and climbs to the B7 arpeggio (B D# F# A),
// the only bar that reaches up. Phrase two goes higher (Am up to e3), then the
// turnaround drops a tenth to low B and climbs back C C# D D# into E, the
// biggest gesture in the riff, saved for the end of the loop.
//
// Variations. A riff played four times the same way stops being heard, so
// the form is  riff - answer - drive - riff - end, over the same eight chords
// every time. Change follows one rule: the first bar of each phrase is kept,
// so the listener always knows where they are, and the variation grows
// toward the phrase end, where the ear expects something to happen.
//
//   answer  the riff, reworded. Bars 1, 3 and 6 are as they were. Bar 2 moves
//           the octave pop onto the offbeat; bar 4 fills the B7 with a b9 (C)
//           before it steps down; bar 5 hammers G onto A and slides home; bar 7
//           reaches F, the flat second of E - the darkest note in the key -
//           before falling back; bar 8 climbs chromatically in octave jumps.
//   drive   the same chords in straight eighths on the root, ghosts pushing
//           beats 1 and 3, the octave on beat 2. Nothing melodic - it is the
//           riff's engine with the tune taken out, so the tune coming back
//           afterward sounds like an arrival. Each bar still ends on a note
//           that walks into the next root.
//
// Pocket. Where the line sits against the beat changes with what the section
// is for. The riff plays on the beat. The answer lays back - it is the
// reflective half, the one with the dark F in it, and behind the beat is
// where a line sounds heavy. The drive pushes ahead, which is what makes
// straight eighths urgent instead of mechanical. Then the riff returns on the
// beat, and after the push that reads as landing.

// Drums. The kit is written as three voices - right hand, left hand, feet -
// because that is what a drummer is, and because a hi-hat over a kick is two
// things at once. What it plays follows from the bass and the form, never
// from a stock beat:
//
//   The kick is the bass's other half. Every kick lands on a bass note, and
//   mostly on the ones that carry weight: the accented root on 1, the root's
//   return on the "and" of 2, the start of the walk on 3. It never doubles a
//   muted ghost, so the ghosts stay ghosts. Where the bass picks a chromatic
//   approach into the next bar (C# into D), the kick pushes with it.
//
//   The snare ghosts on the "a" of 1 and 3, the same sixteenth the bass mutes
//   - the two instruments share the riff's one syncopation instead of
//   competing over it.
//
//   Cymbals mark the hypermeter. Eight-bar sections are two four-bar
//   phrases, and bar 1 outranks bar 5, so bar 1 gets the big crash and bar 5
//   the smaller one. Fills scale with the boundary they cross: one beat at
//   the end of a phrase, two at the end of a section.
//
//   Each fill is the bass's gesture, played on drums. Bar 4's toms fall
//   high to low as the B7 arpeggio falls back to E. Bar 8's snare runs in
//   sixteenths and gets louder under the chromatic climb, with a kick on
//   every climbing note. In the answer, the climb jumps octaves, so the fill
//   alternates floor tom and high tom - low string, high string.
//
//   Each section has its own groove, chosen for what the section does. The
//   riff is a rock backbeat on the hats. The answer goes half-time on the
//   ride, with the snare on 3 only: it lays back, and half-time is the
//   heaviest way to play the same tempo. The drive is four on the floor with
//   open hats on every offbeat, the dance-rock engine under Persona's
//   battle themes, and it moves to the china to sound like a different room.

// Guitar. Double-tracked - two takes panned apart - through the amp in
// tools/build_guitar_sfz.py.
//
//   Power chords, root and fifth and octave, never a third. Distortion adds
//   the sum and difference of every pair of notes it is given: a fifth's
//   difference tone is the root an octave down, which only makes the chord
//   bigger, while a third's is a note outside the chord. So the guitar
//   plays the progression's roots and fifths, and the bass and the B7's
//   leading tone carry the quality of each chord.
//
//   The groove is the 3-3-3-3-2-2 grouping of the bar's sixteen sixteenths:
//   chords on 1, the "a" of 1, the "and" of 2, the "e" of 3, then 4 and its
//   "and". Four groups of three against a beat of four is a small
//   polyrhythm - a dotted-eighth pulse fighting the bar - and the two
//   groups of two put it back on the grid in time for the next downbeat.
//   Tension and release, every bar. The first three accents land with the
//   bass's motif and the kick; the fourth pulls against the bass's straight
//   walk. Between the accents, palm-muted chugs on the root keep the
//   sixteenths moving, and the rest on beat 2 leaves the snare alone.
//
//   Each section's part follows its drums. In the answer the guitar goes
//   half-time too - long chords that ring, so the bass's reworded line is
//   the thing in front. In the drive it plays the bass's own gallop, chords
//   on its accents: rhythmic unison is the loudest thing three instruments
//   can do together. At the end of every section all three climb in unison,
//   C C# D D# into E, since a boundary is where a band plays as one.

// Synths. Both are sfizz's own oscillators - samples/synth/ - and they arrive
// one at a time, so the form is additive: the first riff is the band alone,
// the answer adds the pad, the drive adds the arpeggio, and the reprise is
// the first riff again, note for note (`from:riff`), with both on top. The
// ear hears the same eight bars get bigger, which is the whole trick of a
// Kozuka battle loop.
//
//   The pad is where the chords get their quality. The guitar has no thirds
//   and the bass walks, so the pad holds the third, the seventh and the
//   leading tone, voiced between F#3 and E4 - above the guitar's chords,
//   below the arpeggio - and led like four-part harmony: G B E, G C E
//   (B up a half step to C), F# C D (a D7 - the F#-C tritone wants to
//   resolve, and does, onto B and D#), then F# B D#. The second phrase
//   swaps D for Am and closes on A B D#, a B7 with the fifth left out so the
//   A can fall to G and the D# rise to E - the two half steps that make a
//   dominant resolve. Bars 4 and 8, the turnarounds, swell into the downbeat.
//
//   The arpeggio plays the guitar's groove, not its own. Sixteenths in cells
//   of three - root, third or fifth, octave - grouped 3-3-3-3-2-2 with an
//   accent on each group, so the pluck's accents land exactly on the
//   guitar's chords and the polyrhythm is doubled an octave and a half up.
//   The last bar's second half joins the unison climb in octaves.
//
//   The end chord adds F#, the ninth, over E and B: Em(add9), a minor chord
//   that does not sound finished - the loop's colour, kept to the last.

// The hook. A third synth, Wire (samples/synth/wire-lead.sfz), sings a
// melody over the reprise only - the last layer of the additive form, and
// the one the ear takes away. It replaces nothing: under it the reprise is
// still the first riff, note for note.
//
//   It is made of the riff. Bar 1 is the bass's own opening walk, E G A G,
//   lifted two octaves and given the guitar's dotted-eighth pulse (onsets at
//   0, 0.75, 1.5 and 2.25 beats - the 3-3-3-3 of the guitar's groove), so the
//   hook and the riff are heard as one idea at two heights. Bar 3 is the
//   same shape a step up over D - a sequence, the oldest way to make a
//   melody memorable: the ear learns bar 1 and then recognises bar 3.
//   Bar 5 repeats bar 1 exactly, so the second phrase starts where the
//   first did.
//
//   Long notes land on the chord tones that give the chords their colour,
//   which the guitar leaves out: E, the third of C, in bar 2; D#, the
//   leading tone, held under the B7 in bars 4 and 8. Short notes move by
//   step between them.
//
//   The second phrase borrows the answer's darkness. Bar 6 falls B Bb into
//   A with the bass, a chromatic line in octaves. Bar 7 is the peak - up to
//   C6 over Am - and on the way down it touches F, the flat second, the
//   note the answer made the darkest in the key. Bar 8 holds D# and then
//   joins the band's unison climb, C C# D D# into E, so at the section's
//   end every instrument plays the same gesture.
//
//   Then the whole form again. The second time through (riff2 to reprise2)
//   the riff and the answer are varied around their fixed melodies - see
//   the sections themselves - and the drive leaves the hint out - the ear knows
//   the hook by then, and a fragment of something already heard in full is
//   only a delay - so the second drive is the band and the arpeggio alone,
//   and the hook's return lands straight out of it.
//
//   The last reprise is the only new music: twin leads. A second voice plays
//   the hook in harmony, mostly a diatonic third above, the way two guitars
//   harmonise a melody in a Kozuka finale - the tune enhanced, not replaced.
//   Thirds in E harmonic minor move between major and minor as the scale
//   dictates, and over D the upper voice takes C natural, the D7's seventh,
//   which the pad is already holding. Where the melody moves quickly by step
//   against a long chord (bar 2, the B7 of bar 4) the harmony holds a chord
//   tone and lets the melody move under it, rather than chase it in
//   parallel. Bar 6 holds D over the melody's chromatic B Bb, then both fall
//   to the Am of bar 7, whose peak the harmony tops with E6. In the last bar
//   the voices split: the harmony holds F#, the B7's fifth, then climbs with
//   the band an octave above the melody.
//
//   A hint comes first. In the drive, twice, the lead plays only bar 1's
//   four notes, quietly, and stops. Heard there it is a fragment; heard in
//   full over the reprise it is an arrival, the answer to something the ear
//   has already been given.
//
//   It loops. In a game this is a piece that plays until the player moves
//   on, so the whole form is in brackets and `end` is the way out. The join
//   is one the piece already makes: the last reprise ends on the band's
//   climb to D#, the leading tone over B7, and the riff starts on E - the
//   dominant resolving to the tonic, every time round.

// The bridge. By the first reprise the loop has played the same eight
// chords four times over, and everything has been added that can be added.
// So the one new section takes things away, and it goes somewhere the
// piece has not been, without leaving its key's darkness:
//
//   Harmony. The reprise's climb ends on D#, the leading tone, and every
//   other time it lands on E. Here it lands on C - a deceptive cadence, V
//   to bVI, the ear told home is coming and given the dark side of it
//   instead. From there the bass falls by half steps, C B Bb A: a lament
//   line, the oldest sound of grief, and the opposite of the riff, whose
//   lines all climb. The chords over it are C, G/B, then G minor over Bb -
//   the same chord darkened, the Bb being the tritone of E - and Am. Then
//   Fmaj7, F being E's flat second, the answer's darkest note made into a
//   whole chord for two bars, and B7sus4 resolving to B7 to go home.
//
//   Texture. A breakdown. The guitar plays one chord to mark the landing and
//   leaves; the drums are a kick on 1, a sidestick on 3 and a small ride
//   counting quarters, all quiet. The bass leaves its riff register and
//   holds long notes an octave up. The arpeggio slows to eighths. That
//   leaves the pad and the lead in front for the first time.
//
//   The lead gets a melody of its own, slow where the hook is busy - long
//   notes that move when the chord does. It enters on E, the climb's D#
//   resolving the way a lead line should even as the chord under it
//   refuses to. Bars 2 and 3 are the same phrase a half step apart, B A G
//   and Bb A G, so the G minor is heard as the major chord turning. Over
//   the F it holds E, the major seventh, then touches B, the tritone.
//
//   The build. Bars 7 and 8 bring the band back: palm-muted eighths on B,
//   hats, the snare in eighths then sixteenths, all four drum voices
//   getting louder across the section, and bar 8 is the riff's own last
//   bar - the climb, C C# D D#, in unison - so riff2 arrives exactly as the
//   riff always has. The loop goes riff answer drive reprise, the bridge,
//   then the whole first half again with the twin leads to finish.

// The ending. `end` is the way out of the loop, and a game can take it at
// the end of a pass, at the next section or at the next bar - so it has to
// land from anywhere. It does that by starting where everything already
// points: a hit on E, the tonic, on a downbeat. After the last reprise that
// is the climb's D# resolving; cut in mid-riff it is the band stopping dead
// on the key.
//
// Then nothing new. The chord is the loop's own dark one, Em(add9), with no
// cadence and no turn to major: the piece has been in E minor the whole
// time, and an ending that suddenly celebrates would change what it was.
// The band hits once and lets it ring for two bars; the lead holds E and
// falls off it; and over the decay the arpeggio plays the hook's first four
// notes, E G A G, quietly - the hint from the drive, heard once more as the
// thing the room is left with. It stops on G, the minor third.

title: Low End
ensemble: band
parts: lead harmony:lead@lead guitar keys bass pad perc
tempo: 150
meter: 4/4
key: e minor
order: [riff answer drive reprise bridge riff2 answer2 drive2 reprise2] end

section riff bars:8
  bass dyn:mf..f feel:tight art:marcato
    // Roots and octaves under the guitar's power chords, locked to the kick
    // on 1 and the and of 2. No thirds: the guitar has no third either, and
    // the open fifth is what keeps the harmony from settling.
    | e2:0.5! e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5 b2:0.5 e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5/ |
    // C - the same figure moved, because the riff is the thing that repeats
    | c2:0.5! c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5 g2:0.5 c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5/ |
    // D - and again; the fifth is the only note that is not the root
    | d2:0.5! d2:0.5 d1:0.5! d2:0.5 a2:0.5 d2:0.5 d1:0.5! d2:0.5/ |
    // B - the tritone against it, Bb, taken as a stab on the way back to E
    // rather than a walk: the dominant is coloured, never resolved politely
    | b1:0.5! b1:0.5 b1:0.5 f#2:0.5 b1:0.5! bb1:0.5! b1:0.5 b1:0.5/ |
    // Em - unchanged, so the figure is recognised
    | e2:0.5! e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5 b2:0.5 e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5/ |
    | c2:0.5! c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5 g2:0.5 c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5/ |
    // Am - the lift, still roots and fifths
    | a1:0.5! a1:0.5 a2:0.5! a1:0.5 e2:0.5 a1:0.5 a2:0.5! a1:0.5/ |
    // The turn: sixteenths on B, then the guitar's own chromatic climb
    // C C# D D# doubled underneath it, driving into the next E
    | b1:0.25! b1:0.25 b1:0.25 b1:0.25 b1:0.5 b2:0.5! c2:0.5! c#2:0.5! d2:0.5! d#2:0.5! |

  guitar dyn:mf..f feel:tight art:marcato
    | [e2 b2 e3]:0.5! e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! r:0.25 e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! e2:0.25+ e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! e2:0.25+ e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.5! [e2 b2 e3]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [c3 g3 c4]:0.5! c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! r:0.25 c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! c3:0.25+ c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! c3:0.25+ c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.5! [c3 g3 c4]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [d3 a3 d4]:0.5! d3:0.25+ [d3 a3 d4]:0.25! r:0.25 d3:0.25+ [d3 a3 d4]:0.25! d3:0.25+ d3:0.25+ [d3 a3 d4]:0.25! d3:0.25+ d3:0.25+ [d3 a3 d4]:0.5! [d3 a3 d4]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [b2 f#3 b3]:0.5! b2:0.25+ [b2 f#3 b3]:0.25! r:0.25 b2:0.25+ [b2 f#3 b3]:0.25! b2:0.25+ b2:0.25+ [b2 f#3 b3]:0.25! b2:0.25+ b2:0.25+ [b2 f#3 b3]:0.5! [b2 f#3 b3]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [e2 b2 e3]:0.5! e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! r:0.25 e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! e2:0.25+ e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.25! e2:0.25+ e2:0.25+ [e2 b2 e3]:0.5! [e2 b2 e3]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [c3 g3 c4]:0.5! c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! r:0.25 c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! c3:0.25+ c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.25! c3:0.25+ c3:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.5! [c3 g3 c4]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [a2 e3 a3]:0.5! a2:0.25+ [a2 e3 a3]:0.25! r:0.25 a2:0.25+ [a2 e3 a3]:0.25! a2:0.25+ a2:0.25+ [a2 e3 a3]:0.25! a2:0.25+ a2:0.25+ [a2 e3 a3]:0.5! [a2 e3 a3]:0.25' r:0.25 |
    | [b2 f#3 b3]:0.5! b2:0.25+ b2:0.25+ [b2 f#3 b3]:0.5' b2:0.25+ b2:0.25+ [c3 g3 c4]:0.5! [c#3 g#3 c#4]:0.5! [d3 a3 d4]:0.5! [d#3 a#3 d#4]:0.5! |

  perc.right dyn:mf..f feel:tight
    | crash_big:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. r:1 |
    | crash:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. |
    | hat:0.5! hat:0.5. hat:0.5 hat:0.5. r:2 |
  perc.left dyn:mf..f feel:tight
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:1 snare:0.25! tom_high:0.25 tom_mid:0.25 tom_floor:0.25 |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! r:0.75 snare:0.25. snare:1! |
    | r:0.5 snare:0.25. snare:0.25. snare:1! snare:0.25. snare:0.25. snare:0.25 snare:0.25 snare:0.25 snare:0.25 snare:0.25! snare:0.25! |
  perc.feet dyn:mf..f feel:tight
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:1.5 kick:0.5 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:2 |
    | kick:1.5! kick:0.5 kick:0.5 kick:0.5 kick:0.5 kick:0.5 |

  // Barely there, and two notes only: the third and the seventh, which is
  // all the guitar's fifths leave unsaid. It is the room the riff is played
  // in rather than a part, and it has somewhere to grow to in `answer`.
  pad dyn:ppp..pp
    | [g3 e4]:8 | [g3 e4]:8 |
    | [f#3 d4]:8 | [f#3 d#4]:8^ |

// The riff, reworded. See "Variations" above.
section answer bars:8
  bass dyn:mf..f feel:tight pocket:lay art:marcato
    // The same roots, said later and with more air: the octave lands on the
    // and of 2 instead of the downbeat, so the figure leans back.
    | e2:0.5! e2:0.5 e2:0.25+ e1:0.25! e2:0.5 b2:0.5 e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5/ |
    | c2:0.5! c2:0.5 c2:0.25+ c1:0.25! c2:0.5 g2:0.5 c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5/ |
    | d2:0.5! d2:0.5 d2:0.25+ d1:0.25! d2:0.5 a2:0.5 d2:0.5 d1:0.5! d2:0.5/ |
    // B, and the tritone twice this time, a bar that will not settle
    | b1:0.5! b1:0.5 f#2:0.5 b1:0.5 bb1:0.5! b1:0.5 bb1:0.5! b1:0.5/ |
    | e2:0.5! e2:0.5 e2:0.25+ e1:0.25! e2:0.5 b2:0.5 e2:0.5 e1:0.5! e2:0.5/ |
    | c2:0.5! c2:0.5 c2:0.25+ c1:0.25! c2:0.5 g2:0.5 c2:0.5 c1:0.5! c2:0.5/ |
    // Am, the lift, with the fifth answered an octave up
    | a1:0.5! a1:0.5 a2:0.5! a1:0.5 e2:0.5 e3:0.5' a2:0.5! a1:0.5/ |
    // The turn, the climb doubled with the guitar, octaves on every step
